lationship":"Familiar taught/practice content; the corresponding lesson guide teaches this procedure. No unseen capacity claimed."},"source":null}},{"itemId":"1b814e88-9e97-5b0c-a34d-9964eb102412","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P14","context":{"schemaVersion":"haiedu-authoring-review-v1","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P14","version":1,"locale":"en","sourcePacket":"docs/content/english-7-unit-01-draft.json","sourceReference":"Grigoryan, English 7, Zangak 2023; pp. 13; activities 14; programme T1.K3; docs/content/english-7-unit-01-draft.json#ENG7-U01-P14","hint":"Identify who has the feeling and what causes it.","feedback":"Interested names the learner’s feeling; boring describes the talk’s effect.","optionFeedback":[],"fieldFeedback":[{"id":"f1","text":"Interested describes the person experiencing the feeling.","rejectedExamples":["interesting"]},{"id":"f2","text":"Boring describes the cause of the feeling.","rejectedExamples":["bored"]}],"comparison":{"claim":"ed_ing_roles","scenario":"Choose the word that fits each sentence.","requiredInference":"Use the experiencer/cause relationship, not a person/thing rule.","solution":"Interested names the learner’s feeling; boring describes the talk’s effect.","nearest":["ENG7-U01-P15"],"disposition":"new_reasoning_coverage","rationale":"Use the experiencer/cause relationship, not a person/thing rule.","existingBankMatches":[],"guideRelationship":"Familiar taught/practice content; the corresponding lesson guide teaches this procedure. No unseen capacity claimed."},"source":null}},{"itemId":"55df5dfd-e207-57db-a870-c8c15f64ebd8","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P15","context":{"schemaVersion":"haiedu-authoring-review-v1","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P15","version":1,"locale":"en","sourcePacket":"docs/content/english-7-unit-01-draft.json","sourceReference":"Grigoryan, English 7, Zangak 2023; pp. 13; activities 14; programme T1.K3; docs/content/english-7-unit-01-draft.json#ENG7-U01-P15","hint":"Ask whose feeling the sentence explains.","feedback":"An interesting speaker makes listeners interested. A person can be described with an -ing adjective.","optionFeedback":[{"key":"a","text":"She causes interest in her listeners, so interesting fits."},{"key":"b","text":"Interest is not the adjective required here."},{"key":"c","text":"Interested would describe her own feeling, which is not the supplied point."}],"fieldFeedback":[],"comparison":{"claim":"ed_ing_roles","scenario":"Nare tells lively stories that hold everyone’s attention. In this sentence, Nare is an ____ speaker.","requiredInference":"Apply the same cause/experiencer rule when the cause is a person; repairs the false people=-ed shortcut.","solution":"An interesting speaker makes listeners interested. A person can be described with an -ing adjective.","nearest":["ENG7-U01-P14"],"disposition":"intentional_practice_variant","rationale":"Apply the same cause/experiencer rule when the cause is a person; repairs the false people=-ed shortcut.","existingBankMatches":[],"guideRelationship":"Familiar taught/practice content; the corresponding lesson guide teaches this procedure. No unseen capacity claimed."},"source":null}},{"itemId":"0071eaf3-75f9-52a5-adba-4e9e1191358e","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P16","context":{"schemaVersion":"haiedu-authoring-review-v1","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P16","version":1,"locale":"en","sourcePacket":"docs/content/english-7-unit-01-draft.json","sourceReference":"Grigoryan, English 7, Zangak 2023; pp. 14; activities 15; programme T1.K3; docs/content/english-7-unit-01-draft.json#ENG7-U01-P16","hint":"Decide whether the sentence describes a problem or a sufficient amount or size.","feedback":"Too small describes a problem; big enough and enough fabric describe sufficiency.","optionFeedback":[],"fieldFeedback":[{"id":"f1","text":"Too marks excess that prevents fastening.","rejectedExamples":["enough"]},{"id":"f2","text":"Enough follows an adjective and shows sufficient size.","rejectedExamples":["too"]},{"id":"f3","text":"Enough comes before a noun to show a sufficient amount.","rejectedExamples":["too"]}],"comparison":{"claim":"too_enough_meaning_position","scenario":"Type too or enough in each gap.","requiredInference":"Connect excess/sufficiency with noun versus adjective placement.","solution":"Too small describes a problem; big enough and enough fabric describe sufficiency.","nearest":["ENG7-U01-P17","ENG7-U01-P18"],"disposition":"new_reasoning_coverage","rationale":"Connect excess/sufficiency with noun versus adjective placement.","existingBankMatches":[],"guideRelationship":"Familiar taught/practice content; the corresponding lesson guide teaches this procedure. No unseen capacity claimed."},"source":null}},{"itemId":"71f28a88-9a2c-5a7a-aad5-d2bb2c39cadf","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P17","context":{"schemaVersion":"haiedu-authoring-review-v1","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P17","version":1,"locale":"en","sourcePacket":"docs/content/english-7-unit-01-draft.json","sourceReference":"Grigoryan, English 7, Zangak 2023; pp. 14; activities 15; programme T1.K3; docs/content/english-7-unit-01-draft.json#ENG7-U01-P17","hint":"Find the subject and verb before placing the description.","feedback":"The bag is light enough to carry.","optionFeedback":[],"fieldFeedback":[],"comparison":{"claim":"too_enough_meaning_position","scenario":"Put the segments in order to make one sentence. Begin with The bag.","requiredInference":"Reconstruct the already taught adjective + enough pattern. A different response control is not new reasoning.","solution":"The bag is light enough to carry.","nearest":["ENG7-U01-P16"],"disposition":"intentional_practice_variant","rationale":"Reconstruct the already taught adjective + enough pattern. A different response control is not new reasoning.","existingBankMatches":[],"guideRelationship":"Familiar taught/practice content; the corresponding lesson guide teaches this procedure. No unseen capacity claimed."},"source":null}},{"itemId":"184273ed-cd85-5c0f-a744-d0db50ebb80d","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P18","context":{"schemaVersion":"hai