of early is earlier.","rejectedExamples":["more early","earliest"]}],"comparison":{"claim":"adverb_comparison","scenario":"Use the comparative form of the word in brackets.","requiredInference":"Form a comparative of manner in an explicit two-way comparison.","solution":"More carefully and earlier compare two performances; do not use a superlative.","nearest":["ENG7-U01-P20","ENG7-U01-P28"],"disposition":"new_reasoning_coverage","rationale":"Form a comparative of manner in an explicit two-way comparison.","existingBankMatches":[],"guideRelationship":"Familiar taught/practice content; the corresponding lesson guide teaches this procedure. No unseen capacity claimed."},"source":null}},{"itemId":"8515d2c1-bd0f-587b-af7a-964e6f60141e","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P20","context":{"schemaVersion":"haiedu-authoring-review-v1","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P20","version":1,"locale":"en","sourcePacket":"docs/content/english-7-unit-01-draft.json","sourceReference":"Grigoryan, English 7, Zangak 2023; pp. 15; activities 16; programme T1.K3; docs/content/english-7-unit-01-draft.json#ENG7-U01-P20","hint":"Use the comparative form, not the form used for the highest result in a group.","feedback":"Better and faster compare the stated performances.","optionFeedback":[],"fieldFeedback":[{"id":"f1","text":"Better is the irregular comparative of well.","rejectedExamples":["weller","best"]},{"id":"f2","text":"Faster is the comparative of fast.","rejectedExamples":["more fast","fastest"]}],"comparison":{"claim":"adverb_comparison","scenario":"Complete the comparison using the word in brackets.","requiredInference":"Rehearse comparative adverbs with taught irregular and short forms; more morphology practice, not a new inference.","solution":"Better and faster compare the stated performances.","nearest":["ENG7-U01-P19"],"disposition":"intentional_practice_variant","rationale":"Rehearse comparative adverbs with taught irregular and short forms; more morphology practice, not a new inference.","existingBankMatches":[],"guideRelationship":"Familiar taught/practice content; the corresponding lesson guide teaches this procedure. No unseen capacity claimed."},"source":null}},{"itemId":"73f976b7-8e14-5425-ad1e-10a53d21a2ec","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P21","context":{"schemaVersion":"haiedu-authoring-review-v1","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P21","version":1,"locale":"en","sourcePacket":"docs/content/english-7-unit-01-draft.json","sourceReference":"Grigoryan, English 7, Zangak 2023; pp. 15; activities 16; programme T1.K3; docs/content/english-7-unit-01-draft.json#ENG7-U01-P21","hint":"Check both the comparison form and the word that strengthens it.","feedback":"Much more carefully is the taught intensified comparative.","optionFeedback":[{"key":"a","text":"Most carefully is superlative and does not fit than before."},{"key":"b","text":"Very does not directly strengthen this comparative."},{"key":"c","text":"Much can strengthen the comparative more carefully."}],"fieldFeedback":[],"comparison":{"claim":"comparative_intensifier","scenario":"Which phrase correctly completes this sentence? After practising, I work ____ than before.","requiredInference":"Choose an intensifier that can modify a comparative; this is different from forming the comparative alone.","solution":"Much more carefully is the taught intensified comparative.","nearest":["ENG7-U01-P19","ENG7-U01-P20"],"disposition":"new_reasoning_coverage","rationale":"Choose an intensifier that can modify a comparative; this is different from forming the comparative alone.","existingBankMatches":[],"guideRelationship":"Familiar taught/practice content; the corresponding lesson guide teaches this procedure. No unseen capacity claimed."},"source":null}},{"itemId":"20c8d0bd-bc09-55a0-a0a6-3235c619dbbd","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P22","context":{"schemaVersion":"haiedu-authoring-review-v1","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P22","version":1,"locale":"en","sourcePacket":"docs/content/english-7-unit-01-draft.json","sourceReference":"Grigoryan, English 7, Zangak 2023; pp. 15; activities 17; programme T1.K3; docs/content/english-7-unit-01-draft.json#ENG7-U01-P22","hint":"Read the example word together with its ending.","feedback":"Wearable, colourful, active and stylish are adjectives. Designer and development are nouns. Actively is an adverb. Communicate is a verb. A word class and a meaning such as “able to be” must not compete as if only one were true.","optionFeedback":[],"fieldFeedback":[],"comparison":{"claim":"suffix_function","scenario":"Match each ending, as used in the example, to the word class of the completed word. Categories may be reused. These examples do not define every use of an ending.","requiredInference":"Classify word functions in supplied examples using mutually exclusive word-class categories.","solution":"Wearable, colourful, active and stylish are adjectives. Designer and development are nouns. Actively is an adverb. Communicate is a verb. A word class and a meaning such as “able to be” must not compete as if only one were true.","nearest":["ENG7-U01-P05","ENG7-U01-P06"],"disposition":"new_reasoning_coverage","rationale":"Identify noun, verb, adjective and adverb forms in supplied examples. This repairs both target reuse and the printed mix of overlapping meaning/function categories.","existingBankMatches":[],"guideRelationship":"Familiar taught/practice content; the corresponding lesson guide teaches this procedure. No unseen capacity claimed."},"source":null}},{"itemId":"35b9989a-c368-5d69-a831-0f6ae9c8f259","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-R01","context":{"schemaVersion":"haiedu-authoring-review-v1","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-R01","version":1,"locale":"en","sourcePacket":"docs/content/english-7-unit-01-draft.json","sourceReference":"Grigoryan, English 7, Zangak 2023; pp. 16; activities 18; programme T1.L2; docs/content/english-7-unit-01-draft.json#ENG7-U01-R01","hint":"Locate the time markers in the passage.","feedback":"Ancient societies come first, then the Industrial Revolution, then today.","optionFeedback":[],"fieldFeedback":[],"comparison":{"claim":"reading_chronology","scenario":"According to the passage, put these developments in time order, from earliest to latest.","requiredInference":"Use explicit period markers to order events; no dates or outside historical knowledge required.","solution":"Ancient societies come first, then the Industrial Revolution, then today.","nearest":["ENG7-U01-R02"],"disposition":"new_reasoning_coverage","rationale":"Use explicit period markers to order events; no dates or outside historical knowledge required.","existingBankMatches":[],"guideRelationship":"Familiar taught/practice content; the corresponding lesson guide teaches this procedure. No unseen capacity claimed."},"source":{"id":"READ-HISTORY","kind":"reading","format":"passage","familyId":"77aa9812-745f-577b-ba8d-8430c65b3966","title":"Clothing through time","locale":"en","text":"Clothing has changed over time. In ancient societies such as Egypt, Greece and Rome, clothes could show a person’s social position.\n\nDuring the Industrial Revolution, new manufacturing methods made clothing more widely available. This changed how clothes were produced and bought.\n\nToday, clothes can still express personal choices and cultural traditions. Some designers also think about reducing waste and the environmental effects of making clothes. The history of clothing includes changes in materials, production and people’s choices.","sourceReferences":[],"reviewStatus":"pending","registrationStatus":"unregistered","audioAvailable":false}}},{"itemId":"06865834-6b1a-519f-ab76-0e41df50c749","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-R02","context":{"schemaVersion":"haiedu-authoring-review-v1","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-R02","version":1,"locale":"en","sourcePacket":"docs/content/english-7-unit-01-draft.json","sourceReference":"Grigoryan, English 7, Zangak 2023; pp. 16; activities 18; programme T1.L2; docs/content/english-7-unit-01-draft.json#ENG7-U01-R02","hint":"Consider every paragraph, not only the first example.","feedback":"The whole passage explains changes in clothing over time.","optionFeedback":[{"key":"a","text":"It describes changes rather than sameness."},{"key":"b","text":"The passage moves through several historical periods."},{"key":"c","text":"It gives no sewing instructions."}],"fieldFeedback":[],"comparison":{"claim":"reading_gist","scenario":"Which title best fits the whole passage?","requiredInference":"Identify a whole-text topic across periods, rather than order its events.","solution":"The whole passage explains changes in clothing over time.","nearest":["ENG7-U01-R04"],"disposition":"new_reasoning_coverage","rationale":"Identify a whole-text topic across periods, rather than order its events.","existingBankMatches":[],"guideRelationship":"Familiar taught/practice content; the corresponding lesson guide teaches this procedure. No unseen capacity claimed."},"source":{"id":"READ-HISTORY","kind":"reading","format":"passage","familyId":"77aa9812-745f-577b-ba8d-8430c65b3966","title":"Clothing through time","locale":"en","text":"Clothing has changed over time. In ancient societies such as Egypt, Greece and Rome, clothes could show a person’s social position.\n\nDuring the Industrial Revolution, new manufacturing methods made clothing more widely available. This changed how clothes were produced and bought.\n\nToday, clothes can still express personal choices and cultural traditions. Some designers also think about reducing waste and the environmental effects of making clothes. The history of clothing includes changes in materials, production and people’s choices.","sourceReferences":[],"reviewStatus":"pending","registrationStatus":"unregistered","audioAvailable":false}}},{"itemId":"613c33ee-aebc-54ea-a125-214f63dd4d24","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-R03","context":{"schemaVersion":"haiedu-authoring-review-v1","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-R03","version":1,"locale":"en","sourcePacket":"docs/content/english-7-unit-01-draft.json","sourceReference":"Grigoryan, English 7, Zangak 2023; pp. 16; activities 18; programme T1.L2; docs/content/english-7-unit-01-draft.json#ENG7-U01-R03","hint":"Find the sentence that names the period in the question.","feedback":"The passage links new manufacturing methods with wider availability. It does not claim universal sameness.","optionFeedback":[{"key":"a","text":"The passage makes no claim that everyone dressed alike."},{"key":"b","text":"This is not stated and does not follow from greater availability."},{"key":"c","text":"This is the stated change."}],"fieldFeedback":[],"comparison":{"claim":"reading_period_evidence","scenario":"What change does the passage connect with the Industrial Revolution?","requiredInference":"Locate a change attached to a particular period and reject stronger unsupported claims.","solution":"The passage links new manufacturing methods with wider availability. It does not claim universal sameness.","nearest":["ENG7-U01-R01","ENG7-U01-R02"],"disposition":"new_reasoning_coverage","rationale":"Locate a change attached to a particular period and reject stronger unsupported claims.","existingBankMatches":[],"guideRelationship":"Familiar taught/practice content; the corresponding lesson guide teaches this procedure. No unseen capacity claimed."},"source":{"id":"READ-HISTORY","kind":"reading","format":"passage","familyId":"77aa9812-745f-577b-ba8d-8430c65b3966","title":"Clothing through time","locale":"en","text":"Clothing has changed over time. In ancient societies such as Egypt, Greece and Rome, clothes could show a person’s social position.\n\nDuring the Industrial Revolution, new manufacturing methods made clothing more widely available. This changed how clothes were produced and bought.\n\nToday, clothes can still express personal choices and cultural traditions. Some designers also think about reducing waste and the environmental effects of making clothes. The history of clothing includes changes in materials, production and people’s choices.","sourceReferences":[],"reviewStatus":"pending","registrationStatus":"unregistered","audioAvailable":false}}},{"itemId":"94bdf376-2ab4-53e8-a050-0e4e76094790","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-R04","context":{"schemaVersion":"haiedu-authoring-review-v1","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-R04","version":1,"locale":"en","sourcePacket":"docs/content/english-7-unit-01-draft.json","sourceReference":"Grigoryan, English 7, Zangak 2023; pp. 17, 18; activities 19; programme T1.L2; docs/content/english-7-unit-01-draft.json#ENG7-U01-R04","hint":"Look for the two ideas that the examples help explain.","feedback":"Fashion describes changing trends; personal style describes choices. Neither tells us a person’s whole character.","optionFeedback":[{"key":"a","text":"The opening distinguishes trends from choices."},{"key":"b","text":"The example shows a choice that does not follow the trend."},{"key":"c","text":"The final sentence rejects judging personality from an outfit."}],"fieldFeedback":[],"comparison":{"claim":"reading_gist","scenario":"Which sentence best states the passage’s main distinction?","requiredInference":"Apply familiar gist/contrast reading to the textbook’s style/fashion distinction. It is familiar-topic practice, not unseen reading capacity.","solution":"Fashion describes changing trends; personal style describes choices. Neither tells us a person’s whole character.","nearest":["ENG7-U01-R02","ENG7-U01-P03"],"disposition":"intentional_practice_variant","rationale":"Apply familiar gist/contrast reading to the textbook’s style/fashion distinction. It is familiar-topic practice, not unseen reading capacity.","existingBankMatches":[],"guideRelationship":"Familiar taught/practice content; the corresponding lesson guide teaches this procedure. No unseen capacity claimed."},"source":{"id":"READ-STYLE","kind":"reading","format":"passage","familyId":"caaf26e1-09c1-5a29-8381-f96a038041c8","title":"Fashion and personal style","locale":"en","text":"Fashion describes trends that are popular at a particular time. Personal style is the way someone chooses to dress. A person may use a trend, adapt it or decide not to follow it.\n\nAni notices that bright jackets are popular this month. She keeps wearing her plain shirt because it is comfortable and she likes combining it with different trousers. This is one example of a personal choice.\n\nClassic styles use simple designs that remain popular for a long time. Retro styles draw on clothes from an earlier period. Casual clothes are relaxed choices for everyday activities. These labels describe clothing, not the whole person wearing it. An outfit alone cannot tell us someone’s character.","sourceReferences":[],"reviewStatus":"pending","registrationStatus":"unregistered","audioAvailable":false}}},{"itemId":"4a8f83bb-93c3-50da-addd-f506ff8e8cd6","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-R05","context":{"schemaVersion":"haiedu-authoring-review-v1","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-R05","version":1,"locale":"en","sourcePacket":"docs/content/english-7-unit-01-draft.json","sourceReference":"Grigoryan, English 7, Zangak 2023; pp. 17, 18; activities 19; programme T1.L2; docs/content/english-7-unit-01-draft.json#ENG7-U01-R05","hint":"Find the sentence about Ani’s choice and its reason.","feedback":"Ani values comfort and can combine the shirt with different trousers.","optionFeedback":[{"key":"a","text":"No school rule is mentioned."},{"key":"b","text":"Her choice is contrasted with a current trend."},{"key":"c","text":"Both reasons are directly stated."}],"fieldFeedback":[],"comparison":{"claim":"reading_detail","scenario":"Why does Ani keep wearing her plain shirt?","requiredInference":"Retrieve two connected reasons from a supplied personal-style example.","solution":"Ani values comfort and can combine the shirt with different trousers.","nearest":["ENG7-U01-R06","ENG7-U01-R03"],"disposition":"intentional_practice_variant","rationale":"Further explicit-detail retrieval after R03, now in a personal-choice example. Two stated reasons do not establish an independent new reading process.","existingBankMatches":[],"guideRelationship":"Familiar taught/practice content; the corresponding lesson guide teaches this procedure. No unseen capacity claimed."},"source":{"id":"READ-STYLE","kind":"reading","format":"passage","familyId":"caaf26e1-09c1-5a29-8381-f96a038041c8","title":"Fashion and personal style","locale":"en","text":"Fashion describes trends that are popular at a particular time. Personal style is the way someone chooses to dress. A person may use a trend, adapt it or decide not to follow it.\n\nAni notices that bright jackets are popular this month. She keeps wearing her plain shirt because it is comfortable and she likes combining it with different trousers. This is one example of a personal choice.\n\nClassic styles use simple designs that remain popular for a long time. Retro styles draw on clothes from an earlier period. Casual clothes are relaxed choices for everyday activities. These labels describe clothing, not the whole person wearing it. An outfit alone cannot tell us someone’s character.","sourceReferences":[],"reviewStatus":"pending","registrationStatus":"unregistered","audioAvailable":false}}},{"itemId":"a1b5b7e3-b5da-59a3-af05-12644f0928fe","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-R06","context":{"schemaVersion":"haiedu-authoring-review-v1","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-R06","version":1,"locale":"en","sourcePacket":"docs/content/english-7-unit-01-draft.json","sourceReference":"Grigoryan, English 7, Zangak 2023; pp. 17, 18; activities 19; programme T1.L2; docs/content/english-7-unit-01-draft.json#ENG7-U01-R06","hint":"Return to the paragraph describing the styles.","feedback":"The passage defines retro, casual and classic. These are the same familiar distinctions practised earlier.","optionFeedback":[],"fieldFeedback":[],"comparison":{"claim":"style_meaning","scenario":"Match each description from the passage to its style. Use each style once.","requiredInference":"Explicitly repeated lexical matching in a passage; useful reading-supported retrieval, not an independent new comprehension demand.","solution":"The passage defines retro, casual and classic. These are the same familiar distinctions practised earlier.","nearest":["ENG7-U01-P03","ENG7-U01-R05"],"disposition":"intentional_practice_variant","rationale":"Explicitly repeated lexical matching in a passage; useful reading-supported retrieval, not an independent new comprehension demand.","existingBankMatches":[],"guideRelationship":"Familiar taught/practice content; the corresponding lesson guide teaches this procedure. No unseen capacity claimed."},"source":{"id":"READ-STYLE","kind":"reading","format":"passage","familyId":"caaf26e1-09c1-5a29-8381-f96a038041c8","title":"Fashion and personal style","locale":"en","text":"Fashion describes trends that are popular at a particular time. Personal style is the way someone chooses to dress. A person may use a trend, adapt it or decide not to follow it.\n\nAni notices that bright jackets are popular this month. She keeps wearing her plain shirt because it is comfortable and she likes combining it with different trousers. This is one example of a personal choice.\n\nClassic styles use simple designs that remain popular for a long time. Retro styles draw on clothes from an earlier period. Casual clothes are relaxed choices for everyday activities. These labels describe clothing, not the whole person wearing it. An outfit alone cannot tell us someone’s character.","sourceReferences":[],"reviewStatus":"pending","registrationStatus":"unregistered","audioAvailable":false}}},{"itemId":"61816b25-72d5-5b3a-adc8-125e7c46b706","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-R07","context":{"schemaVersion":"haiedu-authoring-review-v1","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-R07","version":1,"locale":"en","sourcePacket":"docs/content/english-7-unit-01-draft.json","sourceReference":"Grigoryan, English 7, Zangak 2023; pp. 20; activities 21; programme T1.L2; docs/content/english-7-unit-01-draft.json#ENG7-U01-R07","hint":"Check which card supports each statement.","feedback":"The textbook card describes Chanel and comfort. The FDC-based card describes Nikolyan, Armenian traditions and NIKOLYAN.","optionFeedback":[],"fieldFeedback":[],"comparison":{"claim":"reading_source_attribution","scenario":"Use the two source cards. Match each statement to the designer it describes. A name may be used more than once.","requiredInference":"Attribute supplied cultural information to its source card; no memorised designer facts or dates.","solution":"The textbook card describes Chanel and comfort. The FDC-based card describes Nikolyan, Armenian traditions and NIKOLYAN.","nearest":["ENG7-U01-R08","ENG7-U01-R09"],"disposition":"new_reasoning_coverage","rationale":"Attribute supplied cultural information to its source card; no memorised designer facts or dates.","existingBankMatches":[],"guideRelationship":"Familiar taught/practice content; the corresponding lesson guide teaches this procedure. No unseen capacity claimed."},"source":{"id":"READ-DESIGNERS","kind":"reading","format":"passage","familyId":"027e4624-392f-5eb6-8c34-2a502d50e167","title":"Two sources about design","locale":"en","text":"Card A — textbook\nThe textbook describes Coco Chanel as a designer associated with simpler, comfortable clothes. This card gives one example of how a designer can influence clothing.\n\nCard B — Armenian design\nAram Nikolyan is an Armenian designer. His studio is called NIKOLYAN. The Fashion and Design Chamber of Armenia describes his work as drawing on Armenian culture and traditions while creating contemporary clothing. Here, contemporary means belonging to the present time.\n\nRead the cards for information. You do not need to prefer either designer or buy any of their clothes.","sourceReferences":["https://fdc.am/designers/4"],"reviewStatus":"pending","registrationStatus":"unregistered","audioAvailable":false}}},{"itemId":"fb94811b-6365-5203-a06e-fac76afb1153","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-R08","context":{"schemaVersion":"haiedu-authoring-review-v1","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-R08","version":1,"locale":"en","sourcePacket":"docs/content/english-7-unit-01-draft.json","sourceReference":"Grigoryan, English 7, Zangak 2023; pp. 20; activities 21; programme T1.L2; docs/content/english-7-unit-01-draft.json#ENG7-U01-R08","hint":"Choose a statement supported by both cards together.","feedback":"Comfort and cultural traditions are different influences described in the cards. The cards do not rank the designers.","optionFeedback":[{"key":"a","text":"One card mentions comfort and the other Armenian cultural traditions."},{"key":"b","text":"Only the Nikolyan card names NIKOLYAN."},{"key":"c","text":"Neither card makes an expense requirement."}],"fieldFeedback":[],"comparison":{"claim":"reading_cross_source_comparison","scenario":"Which comparison is supported by the two source cards?","requiredInference":"Combine information from two supplied cards to form a limited comparison.","solution":"Comfort and cultural traditions are different influences described in the cards. The cards do not rank the designers.","nearest":["ENG7-U01-R07","ENG7-U01-R09"],"disposition":"new_reasoning_coverage","rationale":"Combine information from two supplied cards to form a limited comparison.","existingBankMatches":[],"guideRelationship":"Familiar taught/practice content; the corresponding lesson guide teaches this procedure. No unseen capacity claimed."},"source":{"id":"READ-DESIGNERS","kind":"reading","format":"passage","familyId":"027e4624-392f-5eb6-8c34-2a502d50e167","title":"Two sources about design","locale":"en","text":"Card A — textbook\nThe textbook describes Coco Chanel as a designer associated with simpler, comfortable clothes. This card gives one example of how a designer can influence clothing.\n\nCard B — Armenian design\nAram Nikolyan is an Armenian designer. His studio is called NIKOLYAN. The Fashion and Design Chamber of Armenia describes his work as drawing on Armenian culture and traditions while creating contemporary clothing. Here, contemporary means belonging to the present time.\n\nRead the cards for information. You do not need to prefer either designer or buy any of their clothes.","sourceReferences":["https://fdc.am/designers/4"],"reviewStatus":"pending","registrationStatus":"unregistered","audioAvailable":false}}},{"itemId":"e16a1958-1645-5755-acc0-f73542782c1a","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-R09","context":{"schemaVersion":"haiedu-authoring-review-v1","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-R09","version":1,"locale":"en","sourcePacket":"docs/content/english-7-unit-01-draft.json","sourceReference":"Grigoryan, English 7, Zangak 2023; pp. 20, 21; activities 22; programme T1.L2; docs/content/english-7-unit-01-draft.json#ENG7-U01-R09","hint":"Read Card B and find the phrase about inspiration.","feedback":"Card B links Nikolyan’s work with Armenian culture and traditions. You do not need to know the designer beforehand.","optionFeedback":[{"key":"a","text":"The cultural connection is explicitly stated."},{"key":"b","text":"Sports uniforms are not the stated source of inspiration."},{"key":"c","text":"No such rule appears."}],"fieldFeedback":[],"comparison":{"claim":"reading_detail","scenario":"According to Card B, what does Aram Nikolyan draw on in his designs?","requiredInference":"Retrieve a fact already used in R07. This deliberate practice variant adds no independent cultural-information coverage.","solution":"Card B links Nikolyan’s work with Armenian culture and traditions. You do not need to know the designer beforehand.","nearest":["ENG7-U01-R07","ENG7-U01-R05"],"disposition":"intentional_practice_variant","rationale":"Retrieve a fact already used in R07. This deliberate practice variant adds no independent cultural-information coverage.","existingBankMatches":[],"guideRelationship":"Familiar taught/practice content; the corresponding lesson guide teaches this procedure. No unseen capacity claimed."},"source":{"id":"READ-DESIGNERS","kind":"reading","format":"passage","familyId":"027e4624-392f-5eb6-8c34-2a502d50e167","title":"Two sources about design","locale":"en","text":"Card A — textbook\nThe textbook describes Coco Chanel as a designer associated with simpler, comfortable clothes. This card gives one example of how a designer can influence clothing.\n\nCard B — Armenian design\nAram Nikolyan is an Armenian designer. His studio is called NIKOLYAN. The Fashion and Design Chamber of Armenia describes his work as drawing on Armenian culture and traditions while creating contemporary clothing. Here, contemporary means belonging to the present time.\n\nRead the cards for information. You do not need to prefer either designer or buy any of their clothes.","sourceReferences":["https://fdc.am/designers/4"],"reviewStatus":"pending","registrationStatus":"unregistered","audioAvailable":false}}},{"itemId":"0f39ed54-3a3b-5d0b-a8d9-b9f38801fed9","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P23","context":{"schemaVersion":"haiedu-authoring-review-v1","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P23","version":1,"locale":"en","sourcePacket":"docs/content/english-7-unit-01-draft.json","sourceReference":"Grigoryan, English 7, Zangak 2023; pp. 22; activities 23; programme T1.L2; docs/content/english-7-unit-01-draft.json#ENG7-U01-P23","hint":"Separate what the writer prefers from a description of an object.","feedback":"The first comment supports a uniform with a time-saving reason; the second opposes it with a choice reason; the third gives a colour detail.","optionFeedback":[],"fieldFeedback":[],"comparison":{"claim":"reading_argument_position","scenario":"Sort the comments by the position they express. You are identifying the writer’s position, not choosing the correct opinion. Categories may be reused.","requiredInference":"Identify an explicitly expressed position separately from a neutral detail; the learner’s own opinion is never marked.","solution":"The first comment supports a uniform with a time-saving reason; the second opposes it with a choice reason; the third gives a colour detail.","nearest":["ENG7-U01-P24"],"disposition":"new_reasoning_coverage","rationale":"Identify an explicitly expressed position separately from a neutral detail; the learner’s own opinion is never marked.","existingBankMatches":[],"guideRelationship":"Familiar taught/practice content; the corresponding lesson guide teaches this procedure. No unseen capacity claimed."},"source":null}},{"itemId":"91d7a0b6-043c-5ee0-a461-412f54bba009","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P24","context":{"schemaVersion":"haiedu-authoring-review-v1","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P24","version":1,"locale":"en","sourcePacket":"docs/content/english-7-unit-01-draft.json","sourceReference":"Grigoryan, English 7, Zangak 2023; pp. 22, 23; activities 24; programme T1.L2; docs/content/english-7-unit-01-draft.json#ENG7-U01-P24","hint":"Look for a choice followed by an explanation of that choice.","feedback":"The first reply explains a preference. A well-supported different preference would also be valid.","optionFeedback":[{"key":"a","text":"Demanding agreement is not a reason for the clothing choice."},{"key":"b","text":"The fragments do not explain the preference."},{"key":"c","text":"The preference and reason are connected."}],"fieldFeedback":[],"comparison":{"claim":"reading_argument_reason","scenario":"Which reply gives a clear preference and a relevant reason?","requiredInference":"Recognise a complete supported preference as preparation for writing; this automatic item does not certify written interaction.","solution":"The first reply explains a preference. A well-supported different preference would also be valid.","nearest":["ENG7-U01-P23"],"disposition":"new_reasoning_coverage","rationale":"Recognise a complete supported preference as preparation for writing; this automatic item does not certify written interaction.","existingBankMatches":[],"guideRelationship":"Familiar taught/practice content; the corresponding lesson guide teaches this procedure. No unseen capacity claimed."},"source":null}},{"itemId":"3ff00d0f-a4d0-570b-a4b9-ef98c4435162","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P25","context":{"schemaVersion":"haiedu-authoring-review-v1","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P25","version":1,"locale":"en","sourcePacket":"docs/content/english-7-unit-01-draft.json","sourceReference":"Grigoryan, English 7, Zangak 2023; pp. 26; activities 29; programme T1.K3; docs/content/english-7-unit-01-draft.json#ENG7-U01-P25","hint":"Check whether the object is new to the reader or already identified.","feedback":"A new jacket; an umbrella; the jacket already introduced. Article choice depends on meaning and sound.","optionFeedback":[],"fieldFeedback":[{"id":"f1","text":"A introduces one unspecified singular countable noun beginning with a consonant sound.","rejectedExamples":["an","the","zero"]},{"id":"f2","text":"An precedes the vowel sound at the start of umbrella.","rejectedExamples":["a","the","zero"]},{"id":"f3","text":"The refers back to the particular jacket already introduced.","rejectedExamples":["a","an","zero"]}],"comparison":{"claim":"articles_reference","scenario":"Type a, an, the or zero. Write zero explicitly when no article is needed.","requiredInference":"Choose indefinite articles by sound and a definite article by an established referent.","solution":"A new jacket; an umbrella; the jacket already introduced. Article choice depends on meaning and sound.","nearest":["ENG7-U01-P26","ENG7-U01-P27"],"disposition":"new_reasoning_coverage","rationale":"Choose indefinite articles by sound and a definite article by an established referent.","existingBankMatches":[],"guideRelationship":"Familiar taught/practice content; the corresponding lesson guide teaches this procedure. No unseen capacity claimed."},"source":null}},{"itemId":"02ac1688-8305-5025-a3fd-1d0754ad49bf","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P26","context":{"schemaVersion":"haiedu-authoring-review-v1","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P26","version":1,"locale":"en","sourcePacket":"docs/content/english-7-unit-01-draft.json","sourceReference":"Grigoryan, English 7, Zangak 2023; pp. 26; activities 29; programme T1.K3; docs/content/english-7-unit-01-draft.json#ENG7-U01-P26","hint":"Use the context to decide whether the noun is general or specific.","feedback":"Zero clothes; the scarf; zero fabric. The supplied contexts remove the need to guess a shared situation.","optionFeedback":[],"fieldFeedback":[{"id":"f1","text":"A general plural noun has no article in this supplied context.","rejectedExamples":["a","an","the"]},{"id":"f2","text":"The phrase identifies the particular scarf.","rejectedExamples":["a","an","zero"]},{"id":"f3","text":"Fabric is an unspecified mass noun here.","rejectedExamples":["a","an","the"]}],"comparison":{"claim":"articles_generic_specific","scenario":"Type a, an, the or zero. Write zero explicitly when no article is needed.","requiredInference":"Contrast general plural/mass reference with a particular identified object. The explicit zero token is a response, not an omitted answer.","solution":"Zero clothes; the scarf; zero fabric. The supplied contexts remove the need to guess a shared situation.","nearest":["ENG7-U01-P25","ENG7-U01-P27"],"disposition":"new_reasoning_coverage","rationale":"Contrast general plural/mass reference with a particular identified object. The explicit zero token is a response, not an omitted answer.","existingBankMatches":[],"guideRelationship":"Familiar taught/practice content; the corresponding lesson guide teaches this procedure. No unseen capacity claimed."},"source":null}},{"itemId":"ee4a4214-735e-5d54-ae3e-d40a3f3d5997","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P27","context":{"schemaVersion":"haiedu-authoring-review-v1","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P27","version":1,"locale":"en","sourcePacket":"docs/content/english-7-unit-01-draft.json","sourceReference":"Grigoryan, English 7, Zangak 2023; pp. 26; activities 29; programme T1.K3; docs/content/english-7-unit-01-draft.json#ENG7-U01-P27","hint":"Say the next word quietly to yourself and attend to its first sound.","feedback":"An old-fashioned coat and a useful bag. The sound controls a/an.","optionFeedback":[],"fieldFeedback":[{"id":"f1","text":"Old-fashioned begins with a vowel sound.","rejectedExamples":["a"]},{"id":"f2","text":"Useful begins with a /y/ consonant sound, so a is used.","rejectedExamples":["an"]}],"comparison":{"claim":"articles_reference","scenario":"Type a or an. Choose by the first sound, not just the first letter.","requiredInference":"Rehearse the indefinite-article rule with a sound/letter contrast; counts as practice of the same rule.","solution":"An old-fashioned coat and a useful bag. The sound controls a/an.","nearest":["ENG7-U01-P25"],"disposition":"intentional_practice_variant","rationale":"Rehearse the indefinite-article rule with a sound/letter contrast; counts as practice of the same rule.","existingBankMatches":[],"guideRelationship":"Familiar taught/practice content; the corresponding lesson guide teaches this procedure. No unseen capacity claimed."},"source":null}},{"itemId":"cb821dcd-ae7b-5ab4-a4e2-10e5bf33ca60","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P28","context":{"schemaVersion":"haiedu-authoring-review-v1","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P28","version":1,"locale":"en","sourcePacket":"docs/content/english-7-unit-01-draft.json","sourceReference":"Grigoryan, English 7, Zangak 2023; pp. 27; activities 30; programme T1.K3; docs/content/english-7-unit-01-draft.json#ENG7-U01-P28","hint":"Count the things being compared in each sentence.","feedback":"Longer compares the pair; longest identifies the maximum within the stated group of three.","optionFeedback":[],"fieldFeedback":[{"id":"f1","text":"The explicit pair requires a comparative.","rejectedExamples":["longest","more long"]},{"id":"f2","text":"The explicit group of three uses the superlative for the greatest length.","rejectedExamples":["longer","most long"]}],"comparison":{"claim":"comparison_scope","scenario":"Use the correct comparative or superlative form of the word in brackets.","requiredInference":"Select comparative versus superlative from an explicitly bounded comparison set; no factual trivia.","solution":"Longer compares the pair; longest identifies the maximum within the stated group of three.","nearest":["ENG7-U01-P19","ENG7-U01-P29","ENG7-U01-P30"],"disposition":"new_reasoning_coverage","rationale":"Select comparative versus superlative from an explicitly bounded comparison set; no factual trivia.","existingBankMatches":[],"guideRelationship":"Familiar taught/practice content; the corresponding lesson guide teaches this procedure. No unseen capacity claimed."},"source":null}},{"itemId":"2829d3ce-637d-50e5-ad03-0ab3c825aeb0","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P29","context":{"schemaVersion":"haiedu-authoring-review-v1","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P29","version":1,"locale":"en","sourcePacket":"docs/content/english-7-unit-01-draft.json","sourceReference":"Grigoryan, English 7, Zangak 2023; pp. 27; activities 30; programme T1.K3; docs/content/english-7-unit-01-draft.json#ENG7-U01-P29","hint":"Use the stated group, not an imagined comparison.","feedback":"More comfortable for the pair; most comfortable for the maximum in the group of four.","optionFeedback":[],"fieldFeedback":[{"id":"f1","text":"Two chairs require the comparative more comfortable.","rejectedExamples":["most comfortable","comfortabler"]},{"id":"f2","text":"The stated group calls for the superlative most comfortable.","rejectedExamples":["more comfortable","comfortablest"]}],"comparison":{"claim":"comparison_scope","scenario":"Use the form of comfortable that fits the stated comparison.","requiredInference":"Practise the same comparison-set decision with a longer adjective, not a new reasoning path.","solution":"More comfortable for the pair; most comfortable for the maximum in the group of four.","nearest":["ENG7-U01-P28"],"disposition":"intentional_practice_variant","rationale":"Practise the same comparison-set decision with a longer adjective, not a new reasoning path.","existingBankMatches":[],"guideRelationship":"Familiar taught/practice content; the corresponding lesson guide teaches this procedure. No unseen capacity claimed."},"source":null}},{"itemId":"0def1d88-a292-5630-a032-50e17775152e","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P30","context":{"schemaVersion":"haiedu-authoring-review-v1","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P30","version":1,"locale":"en","sourcePacket":"docs/content/english-7-unit-01-draft.json","sourceReference":"Grigoryan, English 7, Zangak 2023; pp. 27; activities 30; programme T1.K3; docs/content/english-7-unit-01-draft.json#ENG7-U01-P30","hint":"Use the supplied information and the size of the comparison group.","feedback":"The 1 kg bag is the lightest of the three. All data needed are supplied.","optionFeedback":[{"key":"a","text":"The sentence identifies an extreme in a group, so a comparative alone does not fit."},{"key":"b","text":"One kilogram is the lowest mass in the stated group of three."},{"key":"c","text":"Three kilograms, not one, is the highest mass."}],"fieldFeedback":[],"comparison":{"claim":"comparison_scope","scenario":"Three bags weigh 1 kg, 2 kg and 3 kg. Complete the sentence: Of these three bags, the 1 kg bag is ____.","requiredInference":"Apply the same superlative rule to simple supplied data; no Physics outcome or measurement procedure is assessed.","solution":"The 1 kg bag is the lightest of the three. All data needed are supplied.","nearest":["ENG7-U01-P28","ENG7-U01-P29"],"disposition":"intentional_practice_variant","rationale":"Apply the same superlative rule to simple supplied data; no Physics outcome or measurement procedure is assessed.","existingBankMatches":[],"guideRelationship":"Familiar taught/practice content; the corresponding lesson guide teaches this procedure. No unseen capacity claimed."},"source":null}},{"itemId":"66dec169-854a-55d7-a0a9-18857ffae760","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P32","context":{"schemaVersion":"haiedu-authoring-review-v1","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P32","version":1,"locale":"en","sourcePacket":"docs/content/english-7-unit-01-practice-additions.json","sourceReference":"Grigoryan, English 7, Zangak 2023; pp. 4; activities 2; programme T1.K1; docs/content/english-7-unit-01-practice-additions.json#ENG7-U01-P32","hint":"Choose the word supported by the described behaviour.","feedback":"Sociable fits enjoying group activities and meeting new people. This describes one stated aspect of Lilit, not her whole personality.","optionFeedback":[],"fieldFeedback":[{"id":"f1","text":"Enjoying social contact supports sociable; forgetfulness or nervousness is not described.","rejectedExamples":["shy","absent-minded"]}],"comparison":{"claim":"personality_word_in_context","scenario":"Complete the description with sociable, shy or absent-minded. Use one word.","requiredInference":"Select the personality word supported by the stated social behaviour.","solution":"Sociable fits enjoying group activities and meeting new people. This describes one stated aspect of Lilit, not her whole personality.","nearest":["ENG7-U01-P01","ENG7-U01-P02"],"disposition":"intentional_practice_variant","rationale":"P01 matches other behaviour descriptions and P02 separates appearance from behaviour. This supplies controlled contextual use of sociable; it remains intentional rehearsal of the same inference.","guideRelationship":"G01 teaches evidence-based descriptions; its polite example does not give this answer.","existingProductionEnglishMatches":[],"independentNewReasoningClaimed":false},"source":null}},{"itemId":"c58bfdcf-da70-57dd-aae4-6000bffc9d24","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P33","context":{"schemaVersion":"haiedu-authoring-review-v1","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P33","version":1,"locale":"en","sourcePacket":"docs/content/english-7-unit-01-practice-additions.json","sourceReference":"Grigoryan, English 7, Zangak 2023; pp. 5; activities 4; programme T1.K3; docs/content/english-7-unit-01-practice-additions.json#ENG7-U01-P33","hint":"Decide which gap needs an action word and which needs the name of something.","feedback":"Please describe the jacket; a short description of it. The first gap needs a verb and the second a noun.","optionFeedback":[{"key":"a","text":"Describe expresses the requested action; description names the account."},{"key":"b","text":"This reverses the noun and verb positions."},{"key":"c","text":"The second gap follows a short and needs a noun."},{"key":"d","text":"The first gap follows please and needs the verb."}],"fieldFeedback":[],"comparison":{"claim":"base_and_derived_word_class","scenario":"Which pair completes both lines?\nAram: Please ____ the jacket to me.\nMari: Here is a short ____ of it.","requiredInference":"Distinguish a request for an action from a noun phrase naming the result.","solution":"Please describe the jacket; a short description of it. The first gap needs a verb and the second a noun.","nearest":["ENG7-U01-P05","ENG7-U01-P06","ENG7-U01-P22"],"disposition":"intentional_practice_variant","rationale":"P05 produces description from describe. This reverses between base and derived forms in an exchange and tests both functions; it is a practice variant, not a new independent claim.","guideRelationship":"G02 explains word-class choice with educate/education; no new guide is needed.","existingProductionEnglishMatches":[],"independentNewReasoningClaimed":false},"source":null}},{"itemId":"cd0d0361-3de0-51c6-a19d-b2845d90bef5","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P31","context":{"schemaVersion":"haiedu-authoring-review-v1","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P31","version":1,"locale":"en","sourcePacket":"docs/content/english-7-unit-01-practice-additions.json","sourceReference":"Grigoryan, English 7, Zangak 2023; pp. 5, 6; activities 7; programme T1.K1; docs/content/english-7-unit-01-practice-additions.json#ENG7-U01-P31","hint":"Use the details about the clothes, rather than judging the person wearing them.","feedback":"Flashy describes a look designed to attract attention.","optionFeedback":[],"fieldFeedback":[{"id":"f1","text":"The stated intention is to attract attention, not an earlier period or everyday informality.","rejectedExamples":["casual","retro"]}],"comparison":{"claim":"clothing_style_in_context","scenario":"Complete the clothing description with casual, flashy or retro. Use one word.","requiredInference":"Connect the stated visual features and intention with the supplied style word.","solution":"Flashy describes a look designed to attract attention.","nearest":["ENG7-U01-P01","ENG7-U01-P02","ENG7-U01-P03","ENG7-U01-P04","ENG7-U01-R04","ENG7-U01-R05","ENG7-U01-R06"],"disposition":"intentional_practice_variant","rationale":"P03/R06 recognise style definitions; this deliberately rehearses applying a style word to a concrete verbal description. It is bounded vocabulary production, not independent new reasoning.","guideRelationship":"G03 teaches these style meanings; its retro example is not this jacket description.","existingProductionEnglishMatches":[],"independentNewReasoningClaimed":false},"source":null}},{"itemId":"73bbe712-fbcf-5995-ab9d-eae8282531d3","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-L07","context":{"schemaVersion":"haiedu-authoring-review-v1","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-L07","version":1,"locale":"en","sourcePacket":"docs/content/english-7-unit-01-practice-additions.json","sourceReference":"Grigoryan, English 7, Zangak 2023; pp. 6, 144; activities 8; programme T1.L1; docs/content/english-7-unit-01-practice-additions.json#ENG7-U01-L07","hint":"Think about what connects all three turns.","feedback":"They give different ideas about clothing for a class photo.","optionFeedback":[{"key":"a","text":"They discuss their clothes for a photo, not selecting pictures."},{"key":"b","text":"All three discuss clothing choices for the photo."},{"key":"c","text":"No uniform purchase is required; the speakers propose different choices."}],"fieldFeedback":[],"comparison":{"claim":"combined_speaker_listening","scenario":"Listen to all three speakers. What are they mainly discussing?","requiredInference":"Identify the shared purpose across the three viewpoints.","solution":"They give different ideas about clothing for a class photo.","nearest":["ENG7-U01-L01","ENG7-U01-L03","ENG7-U01-L05"],"disposition":"intentional_practice_variant","rationale":"The existing L01/L03/L05 ask for individual gist. This rehearses gist across a short discussion, using a new original practice source; no protected/unseen or independent coverage is claimed.","guideRelationship":"The familiar book activity teaches listening for clothing choices. Existing scripts and all new item feedback were included in the exposure comparison; no protected capacity is claimed.","existingProductionEnglishMatches":[],"independentNewReasoningClaimed":false},"source":{"id":"LISTEN-PRACTICE-GROUP-01","kind":"listening","format":"script","familyId":"4cef63ac-2f30-5622-9de8-4c4ca483e38c","title":"Listening practice: three speakers","locale":"en","text":"narrator: Listen to three students. You will hear each speaker’s number.\n\nspeaker_1: Speaker One. For our class photo, I’d like us to wear the same colour. We don’t need to buy anything. I already have a blue shirt, and I can wear that. Different shirts are fine if we all choose blue.\n\nspeaker_2: Speaker Two. I’d rather let everyone choose their own colour for the photo. I want to wear my green top. We can look like a group without wearing the same thing.\n\nspeaker_3: Speaker Three. For me, the important thing is to feel comfortable. We’ll be standing outside for a while. I’m going to wear my warm coat over my shirt.","sourceReferences":[],"reviewStatus":"pending","registrationStatus":"unregistered","audioAvailable":false}}},{"itemId":"3c05a599-565c-5e15-a5f2-e5c6b1da8cdc","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-L08","context":{"schemaVersion":"haiedu-authoring-review-v1","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-L08","version":1,"locale":"en","sourcePacket":"docs/content/english-7-unit-01-practice-additions.json","sourceReference":"Grigoryan, English 7, Zangak 2023; pp. 6, 144; activities 8; programme T1.L1; docs/content/english-7-unit-01-practice-additions.json#ENG7-U01-L08","hint":"Listen for the clothing item mentioned in the second turn.","feedback":"Speaker Two says they want to wear a green top.","optionFeedback":[{"key":"a","text":"Speaker One mentions a blue shirt."},{"key":"b","text":"Speaker Three mentions a warm coat."},{"key":"c","text":"Speaker Two explicitly names a green top."}],"fieldFeedback":[],"comparison":{"claim":"combined_speaker_listening","scenario":"Which item does Speaker Two plan to wear?","requiredInference":"Retrieve the explicit clothing detail from the correct numbered turn.","solution":"Speaker Two says they want to wear a green top.","nearest":["ENG7-U01-L02","ENG7-U01-L04","ENG7-U01-L06"],"disposition":"intentional_practice_variant","rationale":"L02/L04/L06 retrieve details from named separate clips. This locates a detail in a combined recording; the detail operation remains familiar practice.","guideRelationship":"The familiar book activity teaches listening for clothing choices. Existing scripts and all new item feedback were included in the exposure comparison; no protected capacity is claimed.","existingProductionEnglishMatches":[],"independentNewReasoningClaimed":false},"source":{"id":"LISTEN-PRACTICE-GROUP-01","kind":"listening","format":"script","familyId":"4cef63ac-2f30-5622-9de8-4c4ca483e38c","title":"Listening practice: three speakers","locale":"en","text":"narrator: Listen to three students. You will hear each speaker’s number.\n\nspeaker_1: Speaker One. For our class photo, I’d like us to wear the same colour. We don’t need to buy anything. I already have a blue shirt, and I can wear that. Different shirts are fine if we all choose blue.\n\nspeaker_2: Speaker Two. I’d rather let everyone choose their own colour for the photo. I want to wear my green top. We can look like a group without wearing the same thing.\n\nspeaker_3: Speaker Three. For me, the important thing is to feel comfortable. We’ll be standing outside for a while. I’m going to wear my warm coat over my shirt.","sourceReferences":[],"reviewStatus":"pending","registrationStatus":"unregistered","audioAvailable":false}}},{"itemId":"db0b731b-30b7-54a4-a5e1-5a8f76a946f8","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-L09","context":{"schemaVersion":"haiedu-authoring-review-v1","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-L09","version":1,"locale":"en","sourcePacket":"docs/content/english-7-unit-01-practice-additions.json","sourceReference":"Grigoryan, English 7, Zangak 2023; pp. 6, 144; activities 8; programme T1.L1; docs/content/english-7-unit-01-practice-additions.json#ENG7-U01-L09","hint":"Follow the numbered turns and note each speaker’s proposal.","feedback":"Speaker One suggests that everyone choose blue, while allowing different shirts.","optionFeedback":[{"key":"a","text":"Speaker One suggests choosing blue."},{"key":"b","text":"Speaker Two wants people to choose their own colour."},{"key":"c","text":"Speaker Three focuses on comfort and a warm coat."}],"fieldFeedback":[],"comparison":{"claim":"combined_speaker_listening","scenario":"Who suggests that the group should choose one colour?","requiredInference":"Attribute the shared-colour proposal to its numbered speaker.","solution":"Speaker One suggests that everyone choose blue, while allowing different shirts.","nearest":["ENG7-U01-L02","ENG7-U01-L04","ENG7-U01-L06","ENG7-U01-L10"],"disposition":"intentional_practice_variant","rationale":"The old six questions use separately named clips, so they do not establish speaker discrimination. This directly rehearses attribution within one recording. L10 tests the same attribution operation on another stated view; these are not independent source samples.","guideRelationship":"The familiar book activity teaches listening for clothing choices. Existing scripts and all new item feedback were included in the exposure comparison; no protected capacity is claimed.","existingProductionEnglishMatches":[],"independentNewReasoningClaimed":false},"source":{"id":"LISTEN-PRACTICE-GROUP-01","kind":"listening","format":"script","familyId":"4cef63ac-2f30-5622-9de8-4c4ca483e38c","title":"Listening practice: three speakers","locale":"en","text":"narrator: Listen to three students. You will hear each speaker’s number.\n\nspeaker_1: Speaker One. For our class photo, I’d like us to wear the same colour. We don’t need to buy anything. I already have a blue shirt, and I can wear that. Different shirts are fine if we all choose blue.\n\nspeaker_2: Speaker Two. I’d rather let everyone choose their own colour for the photo. I want to wear my green top. We can look like a group without wearing the same thing.\n\nspeaker_3: Speaker Three. For me, the important thing is to feel comfortable. We’ll be standing outside for a while. I’m going to wear my warm coat over my shirt.","sourceReferences":[],"reviewStatus":"pending","registrationStatus":"unregistered","audioAvailable":false}}},{"itemId":"6a3b79eb-652e-5b6d-a095-cf32e1e5ff21","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-L10","context":{"schemaVersion":"haiedu-authoring-review-v1","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-L10","version":1,"locale":"en","sourcePacket":"docs/content/english-7-unit-01-practice-additions.json","sourceReference":"Grigoryan, English 7, Zangak 2023; pp. 6, 144; activities 8; programme T1.L1; docs/content/english-7-unit-01-practice-additions.json#ENG7-U01-L10","hint":"Distinguish each speaker’s own view from the other suggestions.","feedback":"Speaker Two says they would rather let everyone choose their own colour.","optionFeedback":[{"key":"a","text":"Speaker One suggests the same colour for everyone."},{"key":"b","text":"Speaker Two explicitly favours each person choosing a colour."},{"key":"c","text":"Speaker Three states a comfort preference rather than this colour proposal."}],"fieldFeedback":[],"comparison":{"claim":"combined_speaker_listening","scenario":"Who wants everyone to choose their own colour?","requiredInference":"Attribute the individual-colour preference to its numbered speaker.","solution":"Speaker Two says they would rather let everyone choose their own colour.","nearest":["ENG7-U01-L02","ENG7-U01-L04","ENG7-U01-L06","ENG7-U01-L09"],"disposition":"intentional_practice_variant","rationale":"This is deliberate second practice of L09’s attribution operation within the same source family, using the contrasting view. It is not a second independent reasoning dimension or unseen sample.","guideRelationship":"The familiar book activity teaches listening for clothing choices. Existing scripts and all new item feedback were included in the exposure comparison; no protected capacity is claimed.","existingProductionEnglishMatches":[],"independentNewReasoningClaimed":false},"source":{"id":"LISTEN-PRACTICE-GROUP-01","kind":"listening","format":"script","familyId":"4cef63ac-2f30-5622-9de8-4c4ca483e38c","title":"Listening practice: three speakers","locale":"en","text":"narrator: Listen to three students. You will hear each speaker’s number.\n\nspeaker_1: Speaker One. For our class photo, I’d like us to wear the same colour. We don’t need to buy anything. I already have a blue shirt, and I can wear that. Different shirts are fine if we all choose blue.\n\nspeaker_2: Speaker Two. I’d rather let everyone choose their own colour for the photo. I want to wear my green top. We can look like a group without wearing the same thing.\n\nspeaker_3: Speaker Three. For me, the important thing is to feel comfortable. We’ll be standing outside for a while. I’m going to wear my warm coat over my shirt.","sourceReferences":[],"reviewStatus":"pending","registrationStatus":"unregistered","audioAvailable":false}}},{"itemId":"c10424d1-4dbe-5d0e-a1f1-6937b3cf20dd","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P35","context":{"schemaVersion":"haiedu-authoring-review-v1","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P35","version":1,"locale":"en","sourcePacket":"docs/content/english-7-unit-01-practice-additions.json","sourceReference":"Grigoryan, English 7, Zangak 2023; pp. 8, 9; activities 9; programme T1.K3; docs/content/english-7-unit-01-practice-additions.json#ENG7-U01-P35","hint":"Keep both the action and the place where it is prohibited.","feedback":"Do not wear your coat indoors preserves the negative instruction and its location.","optionFeedback":[{"key":"a","text":"This reverses the prohibition."},{"key":"b","text":"This preserves both the prohibition and indoors."},{"key":"c","text":"This changes the place where the instruction applies."}],"fieldFeedback":[],"comparison":{"claim":"interpret_negative_instruction","scenario":"Mariam told Aram not to wear his coat indoors. Which direct instruction preserves her meaning?","requiredInference":"Recover the prohibited action and its scope from a reported instruction.","solution":"Do not wear your coat indoors preserves the negative instruction and its location.","nearest":["ENG7-U01-P07","ENG7-U01-P08","ENG7-U01-P09"],"disposition":"intentional_practice_variant","rationale":"P08 supplies a negative infinitive and P09 recognises a reporting pattern. This asks for meaning preservation in the reverse direction; no independent reasoning coverage is claimed.","guideRelationship":"G04 teaches the same negative pattern; its positive door example does not resolve the contrast.","existingProductionEnglishMatches":[],"independentNewReasoningClaimed":false},"source":null}},{"itemId":"4f732a65-405a-54cf-aaac-6166a320a0e4","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P36","context":{"schemaVersion":"haiedu-authoring-review-v1","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P36","version":1,"locale":"en","sourcePacket":"docs/content/english-7-unit-01-practice-additions.json","sourceReference":"Grigoryan, English 7, Zangak 2023; pp. 12; activities 13; programme T1.K3; docs/content/english-7-unit-01-practice-additions.json#ENG7-U01-P36","hint":"Ask whether the word describes the plan or how someone performs an action.","feedback":"Good describes the plan after sounds.","optionFeedback":[],"fieldFeedback":[{"id":"f1","text":"In this sentence sounds links plan to an assessment of its quality; good is the adjective.","rejectedExamples":["well"]}],"comparison":{"claim":"adjective_linking_complement","scenario":"Choose good or well to complete the sentence.","requiredInference":"Identify sounds as linking the plan to a quality, rather than an action being performed well.","solution":"Good describes the plan after sounds.","nearest":["ENG7-U01-P12","ENG7-U01-P13"],"disposition":"intentional_practice_variant","rationale":"P12/P13 contrast feels/looks with action adverbs. This rehearses the textbook sounds pattern and the good/well contrast; it is not additional independent coverage.","guideRelationship":"G05 explains linking-verb complements without using this plan sentence.","existingProductionEnglishMatches":[],"independentNewReasoningClaimed":false},"source":null}},{"itemId":"6df50b22-f02b-596d-a6d0-412d4eeb07fc","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P37","context":{"schemaVersion":"haiedu-authoring-review-v1","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P37","version":1,"locale":"en","sourcePacket":"docs/content/english-7-unit-01-practice-additions.json","sourceReference":"Grigoryan, English 7, Zangak 2023; pp. 12; activities 13; programme T1.K3; docs/content/english-7-unit-01-practice-additions.json#ENG7-U01-P37","hint":"The second sentence describes how the action was performed.","feedback":"Ani explained her choice carefully preserves the meaning of a careful explanation.","optionFeedback":[],"fieldFeedback":[{"id":"f1","text":"Carefully modifies explained; no comparison is supplied, so more carefully changes the meaning.","rejectedExamples":["careful","more carefully"]}],"comparison":{"claim":"adverb_action_modifier","scenario":"Complete the second sentence with careful or carefully so that it keeps the first sentence’s meaning.","requiredInference":"Convert an adjective-noun description into an adverb modifying the action.","solution":"Ani explained her choice carefully preserves the meaning of a careful explanation.","nearest":["ENG7-U01-P12","ENG7-U01-P13","ENG7-U01-P19","ENG7-U01-P20"],"disposition":"intentional_practice_variant","rationale":"P12/P13 select modifiers and P19 forms a comparative adverb. This rehearses the uninflected action modifier through a short paraphrase, without claiming a new reasoning path.","guideRelationship":"G05 teaches adjective/action distinctions; comparison forms from G06 are not required.","existingProductionEnglishMatches":[],"independentNewReasoningClaimed":false},"source":null}},{"itemId":"d54c0eb5-42c5-545b-ae39-3027c9790b97","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P38","context":{"schemaVersion":"haiedu-authoring-review-v1","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P38","version":1,"locale":"en","sourcePacket":"docs/content/english-7-unit-01-practice-additions.json","sourceReference":"Grigoryan, English 7, Zangak 2023; pp. 13; activities 14; programme T1.K3; docs/content/english-7-unit-01-practice-additions.json#ENG7-U01-P38","hint":"Distinguish the cause of the feeling from the person experiencing it.","feedback":"The result was surprising; Tatev was surprised.","optionFeedback":[],"fieldFeedback":[{"id":"f1","text":"The result causes the feeling, so surprising describes it.","rejectedExamples":["surprised"]},{"id":"f2","text":"Tatev experiences the feeling, so surprised describes her here.","rejectedExamples":["surprising"]}],"comparison":{"claim":"contrast_feeling_and_cause","scenario":"Use surprised or surprising in each gap.","requiredInference":"Keep the unexpected result and its experiencer distinct across one linked context.","solution":"The result was surprising; Tatev was surprised.","nearest":["ENG7-U01-P14","ENG7-U01-P15"],"disposition":"intentional_practice_variant","rationale":"P14 uses interested/boring and P15 treats a person as a cause. This applies another taught pair to two linked statements. G05 already teaches the complete procedure; this is deliberate rehearsal only.","guideRelationship":"G05 uses exciting/excited; this mini-context is not that worked example.","existingProductionEnglishMatches":[],"independentNewReasoningClaimed":false},"source":null}},{"itemId":"42de83dc-b896-5dfb-a599-fdeb464ba7d5","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P39","context":{"schemaVersion":"haiedu-authoring-review-v1","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P39","version":1,"locale":"en","sourcePacket":"docs/content/english-7-unit-01-practice-additions.json","sourceReference":"Grigoryan, English 7, Zangak 2023; pp. 14; activities 15; programme T1.K3; docs/content/english-7-unit-01-practice-additions.json#ENG7-U01-P39","hint":"Compare the available number with what is needed for all three coats.","feedback":"Three buttons are needed but only two are available, so there are not enough for all three coats.","optionFeedback":[{"key":"a","text":"Two is fewer than the three required, so this is not an excess."},{"key":"b","text":"Exactly enough would require three buttons."},{"key":"c","text":"The available two buttons cannot replace all three missing buttons."}],"fieldFeedback":[],"comparison":{"claim":"interpret_sufficiency","scenario":"One button is missing from each of three coats. We have two buttons to use. Which sentence correctly describes the situation?","requiredInference":"Use the stated requirement to distinguish shortage from sufficiency and excess.","solution":"Three buttons are needed but only two are available, so there are not enough for all three coats.","nearest":["ENG7-U01-P16","ENG7-U01-P17","ENG7-U01-P18"],"disposition":"intentional_practice_variant","rationale":"P16/P17 complete or order enough constructions; P18 selects much/many. This gives the language a small explicit sufficiency decision. The count is supplied and no mathematical outcome or new independent coverage is claimed.","guideRelationship":"G06 explains sufficiency; its warm-room example does not supply this answer.","existingProductionEnglishMatches":[],"independentNewReasoningClaimed":false},"source":null}},{"itemId":"cdf9d2fa-aed4-5c3c-a1de-a4b1aa1dca98","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P40","context":{"schemaVersion":"haiedu-authoring-review-v1","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P40","version":1,"locale":"en","sourcePacket":"docs/content/english-7-unit-01-practice-additions.json","sourceReference":"Grigoryan, English 7, Zangak 2023; pp. 15; activities 16; programme T1.K3; docs/content/english-7-unit-01-practice-additions.json#ENG7-U01-P40","hint":"Consider the job of far in this whole phrase.","feedback":"Far strengthens warmer: Ani describes a large difference in warmth, not a distance.","optionFeedback":[{"key":"a","text":"Far strengthens the comparison."},{"key":"b","text":"Far modifies warmer here, rather than giving a location."},{"key":"c","text":"Warmer expresses a difference, and far strengthens it."}],"fieldFeedback":[],"comparison":{"claim":"interpret_comparative_intensifier","scenario":"Ani says, “This coat is far warmer than my old one.” What does far add to the comparison?","requiredInference":"Interpret far as a degree modifier rather than its literal distance meaning.","solution":"Far strengthens warmer: Ani describes a large difference in warmth, not a distance.","nearest":["ENG7-U01-P19","ENG7-U01-P20","ENG7-U01-P21","ENG7-U01-P28","ENG7-U01-P29","ENG7-U01-P30"],"disposition":"intentional_practice_variant","rationale":"P21 recognises a grammatical intensifier phrase and P19/P20/P28–P30 form comparisons. This rehearses the meaning of a taught intensifier; the same taught comparison procedure remains shared.","guideRelationship":"G06 teaches much/far with comparatives. No precise temperature or scientific inference is required.","existingProductionEnglishMatches":[],"independentNewReasoningClaimed":false},"source":null}},{"itemId":"a3dd12a9-db0c-5d41-add4-8b0b90a983a1","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P34","context":{"schemaVersion":"haiedu-authoring-review-v1","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P34","version":1,"locale":"en","sourcePacket":"docs/content/english-7-unit-01-practice-additions.json","sourceReference":"Grigoryan, English 7, Zangak 2023; pp. 15; activities 17; programme T1.K3; docs/content/english-7-unit-01-practice-additions.json#ENG7-U01-P34","hint":"Check the spelling of the taught suffix.","feedback":"The adjective is useful, with one final l.","optionFeedback":[],"fieldFeedback":[{"id":"f1","text":"Useful is the requested adjective; usefull is misspelled, usefully is an adverb and use is the base.","rejectedExamples":["usefull","usefully","use"]}],"comparison":{"claim":"produce_taught_derived_adjective","scenario":"Add the suffix -ful to use to complete the sentence with an adjective.","requiredInference":"Build and spell the explicitly taught adjective from its base and suffix.","solution":"The adjective is useful, with one final l.","nearest":["ENG7-U01-P05","ENG7-U01-P06","ENG7-U01-P22"],"disposition":"intentional_practice_variant","rationale":"P22 identifies the word class of colourful; this produces the taught useful form in context. The suffix is specified to exclude other valid derivatives of use from this bounded task.","guideRelationship":"G02 covers formation; the cited p.15 explicitly gives useful as a -ful adjective.","existingProductionEnglishMatches":[],"independentNewReasoningClaimed":false},"source":null}},{"itemId":"fa2119c2-ab55-51a3-a941-9ead8b053d4a","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P41","context":{"schemaVersion":"haiedu-authoring-review-v1","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-P41","version":1,"locale":"en","sourcePacket":"docs/content/english-7-unit-01-practice-additions.json","sourceReference":"Grigoryan, English 7, Zangak 2023; pp. 26; activities 29; programme T1.K3; docs/content/english-7-unit-01-practice-additions.json#ENG7-U01-P41","hint":"Track when a particular scarf becomes identifiable to both speakers.","feedback":"A scarf introduces an unspecified singular scarf. The scarf on the table identifies the particular one the seller has put there.","optionFeedback":[],"fieldFeedback":[{"id":"f1","text":"No particular scarf is yet identified, and scarf begins with a consonant sound.","rejectedExamples":["an","the","zero"]},{"id":"f2","text":"There is one identifiable scarf on the table, shared by both speakers.","rejectedExamples":["a","an","zero"]}],"comparison":{"claim":"first_and_identified_reference","scenario":"Complete the exchange with a, an, the or zero. Ani has not chosen a particular scarf. Type zero explicitly if no article is needed.","requiredInference":"Move from an unspecified request to a shared identifiable referent in an exchange.","solution":"A scarf introduces an unspecified singular scarf. The scarf on the table identifies the particular one the seller has put there.","nearest":["ENG7-U01-P25","ENG7-U01-P26","ENG7-U01-P27"],"disposition":"intentional_practice_variant","rationale":"P25 already introduces and refers back to an object; P26 identifies a scarf by location. This intentionally combines those familiar uses across a dialogue. It is not claimed as new independent article coverage.","guideRelationship":"G09 teaches the same first/subsequent reference procedure; changing the scene alone creates no novelty claim.","existingProductionEnglishMatches":[],"independentNewReasoningClaimed":false},"source":null}},{"itemId":"1ea0570e-fb6c-58b3-aab4-2e950491baff","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-DA-01","context":{"schemaVersion":"haiedu-authoring-review-v1","itemCode":"ENG7-U01-DA-01","version":1,"locale":"en","sourcePacket":"docs/content/english-7-unit-01-assessment-batch-a.json","sourceReference":"Grigoryan, English 7, Zangak 2023; pp. 4, 5, 6; activities 1, 2, 5, 6, 7; programme T1.K1; docs/content/english-7-unit-01-assessment-batch-a.json#ENG7-U01-DA-01","hint":"Use the supplied information and preserve its meaning.","feedback":"Noticing another person''s difficulty and responding supports attentive in this situation.","optionFeedback":[{"key":"a","text":"The student notices the difficulty rather than forgetting or overlooking it."},{"key":"b","text":"Flashy could describe the hat''s look; it does not describe the helpful attention."},{"key":"c","text":"Noticing another person''s difficulty and responding supports attentive in this situation."}],"fieldFeedback":[],"comparison":{"disposition":"protected_parallel_assessment_variant","protocol":"haiedu-protected-parallel-pilot-v1","authorization":"docs/evidence/english-7-unit-01-owner-acceptance-20261021.json","repeatedTaughtClaim":"Personality meaning from stated behaviour","nearestPrac